# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (Render Postgres, or any plain Postgres instance)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The full connection string, including credentials. On Render, copy this
# from your Postgres instance's "Internal Database URL" (when the app and
# the database are both on Render, in the same region -- faster, and no
# egress cost) or "External Database URL" (from anywhere else, e.g. running
# the app locally against a Render database).
#
# SERVER ONLY. Never add a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix. This is imported exclusively
# through src/lib/db/client.ts, which is marked `server-only` so that
# importing it from a client component is a BUILD ERROR rather than a
# silent leak -- same discipline the old Supabase service-role client used.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/dbname

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session signing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the admin session cookie (src/lib/session.ts). Must be at least 32
# random characters -- generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Rotating this value invalidates every existing admin session (everyone is
# signed out) but does not touch any stored password.
AUTH_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Canonical origin, used for metadataBase, sitemap, robots, and invite
# links. Never hardcode a hostname in source -- this is the single switch
# for moving to a custom domain later. Falls back to Render's own
# RENDER_EXTERNAL_URL (set automatically on every Render web service) when
# unset, then to Vercel's equivalent, then to localhost.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Super-admin bootstrap (Phase B)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated emails that are ALWAYS admins, with full access including
# roles (Team) and the theme editor.
#
# A row in admin_credentials (a password) is NOT sufficient on its own --
# every sign-in still passes through this allowlist (or the admin_users
# table) before it counts as admin. See src/lib/auth.ts.
#
# Super-admin status is deliberately never read from a database row -- so a
# super admin here cannot be created, promoted, or removed by anyone who
# only compromises the database. Ordinary admins, invited from /admin/team
# by a super admin, are stored in the admin_users table instead and need no
# env var.
#
# An empty or missing value, combined with an empty/unreachable admin_users
# table, authorizes nobody -- it does not fall open.
#
# A super admin still needs a PASSWORD to sign in -- there is no signup page
# in this app. Set the first one's password with:
#   npm run create-super-admin -- you@example.com
SUPER_ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com
